---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import VideoToMp3Batch from '@/components/tools/media/VideoToMp3Batch.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="mp4-to-mp3">
  <VideoToMp3Batch
    client:load
    from="mp4"
    hint="Drop in as many MP4 files as you need. Each one is decoded on this device"
  />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to pull the audio out of an MP4</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop one file or fifty onto the box above.</li>
      <li>Pick a bitrate. 192 kbps suits music. 128 kbps or mono is enough for speech.</li>
      <li>Press extract. Each file gets its own progress bar and download button.</li>
      <li>With more than one result, download them all at once as a ZIP.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Choosing a bitrate</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Bitrate</th>
            <th>Size per hour</th>
            <th>Good for</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>320 kbps</td>
            <td>~137 MB</td>
            <td>Music you will listen to on decent headphones</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>256 kbps</td>
            <td>~110 MB</td>
            <td>Music, effectively transparent</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>192 kbps</td>
            <td>~82 MB</td>
            <td>The default. Good for almost everything</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>128 kbps</td>
            <td>~55 MB</td>
            <td>Podcasts, lectures, background listening</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>96 kbps mono</td>
            <td>~41 MB</td>
            <td>Interviews and voice notes for transcription</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>About quality</h2>
    <p>
      An MP4 usually carries AAC audio, which is already lossy. MP4 to MP3 compresses the decoded
      result a second time, and no setting can recover detail the first pass discarded. That is
      generation loss, and it comes from the formats, not from this tool.
    </p>
    <p>
      In practice it is inaudible at 192 kbps and above on almost all material. To avoid the second
      compression, extract to M4A with the <a href="/audio-converter">audio converter</a>. An M4A
      extraction can copy the AAC stream out of the video unchanged, which is lossless. Choose MP3 for
      a car stereo, an old MP3 player or software from 2009. Choose M4A when quality is the priority.
    </p>

    <h2>Why a batch queue matters here</h2>
    <p>
      Upload-based converters limit this operation, because each conversion costs them CPU time and
      bandwidth. Here the conversion runs locally, so there is no file counter or daily limit. Queue a
      whole lecture series and leave it to run.
    </p>

    <h2>What is not included</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No ID3 tags. The MP3 has no title, artist or album, and no stray metadata from the source video.</li>
      <li>No normalisation. The loudness of the source stays exactly the same, so a quiet recording stays quiet.</li>
      <li>No trimming. For part of the audio, extract first and then use <a href="/trim-audio">trim audio</a>.</li>
      <li>One audio track. If the MP4 has several languages, the tool uses the first.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>A note on what you convert</h2>
    <p>
      This tool runs on your machine and asks nothing about your file, but the law still applies. To
      extract audio from something you bought or recorded is your business. To redistribute somebody
      else’s work is not.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
